@* The phone home's frame. In the phone shell (MobileLayout) it renders the page's blocks as they are,
   so each stays a DIRECT child of main.scm-content and keeps that container's gap and gutter. The
   web host runs with Platform = Web and renders the same route inside the desktop MainLayout, where
   there is no .scm-shell, and every home style is scoped under .scm-shell. There the blocks get a
   shell of their own: a phone-width column with the same class names, so it looks the same.

   The web frame must never become a stacking context (no isolation, z-index, transform or filter):
   the share sheet, the deity popup and the chant sheet render inside it with z-index 1000, and a
   stacking context here would put the desktop's sticky top bar (z-index 20) above them. That is why
   the aurora is the frame's own background there instead of a fixed layer at z-index -1. *@
@inject AppConfig Config
@inject LocalizationState Loc

@if (Config.IsMobile)
{
    @ChildContent
}
else
{
    <div class="scm-shell scm-webhome" lang="@LangCode">
        <div class="scm-content">
            @ChildContent
        </div>
    </div>
}

@code {
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    // The Indic caption rules key on :lang() of the shell, exactly as MobileLayout sets it.
    private string LangCode => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Loc.CurrentCode) ? LocalizationState.BaseCode : Loc.CurrentCode;
}